    'Cover up at dawn and dusk with loose long sleeves and trousers, and sleep with air-conditioning, window screens or a net.': null,
    'Dengue fever is the most common serious mosquito-borne illness across all four countries. It is spread by day-biting Aedes mosquitoes and rises sharply in the rainy season.': null,
    'Hear its call': null, 'In the sea': null,
    'Japanese encephalitis (rural rice-farming areas in the wet season) and Zika are also present; consider vaccination for long or rural stays, and pregnant travellers should seek specific advice.': null,
    'Know what to avoid and what to do. Tap any animal for a photo, how to identify it, and first aid if you are bitten or stung. If in doubt, keep your distance and get to a hospital.': null,
    'Larger animals': null,
    'Malaria is a risk mainly in rural, forested and some border areas (not the big cities), spread by night-biting mosquitoes — ask a travel clinic about prophylaxis for those regions.': null,
    'Most animals leave you alone if you leave them alone. Wear shoes at night, do not reach into holes or thick leaf litter, and never handle or corner wildlife.': null,
    'Save to my identifier': null, 'Scorpions & centipedes': null,
    'Seasonal guidance only — there is no reliable real-time case feed here. Check your government travel-health advisory and a travel clinic before you go.': null,
    'See a doctor for any high fever, severe headache or aching joints during or after your trip. Dengue needs rest, fluids and monitoring; avoid aspirin and ibuprofen, and get urgent care for warning signs such as bleeding, severe abdominal pain or persistent vomiting.': null,
    'Snakes': null,
    'Tip out or cover any standing water where you are staying (buckets, plant saucers, old tyres) — that is where these mosquitoes breed.': null,
    'Use a repellent with DEET (20-30%) or picaridin on exposed skin and reapply — the Aedes mosquitoes that carry dengue bite by DAY, peaking in early morning and late afternoon.': null,
